.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt MEM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mem
.Nd physical memory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/mem
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /dev/mem
is the physical address space, from 0 to 4 GiB, as Linux gives it with
.Dv STRICT_DEVMEM :
a byte at an offset of the file is the byte at that physical address.
A privileged process reads and writes it with
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr pread 2
and
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
seeks it with
.Xr lseek 2 ,
and maps it with
.Xr mmap 2 ,
where BusyBox's
.Ic devmem
reaches a device's registers.
.Pp
It reaches the first megabyte, the frames the memory map does not offer
as RAM, which firmware and devices have, and the addresses past the top
of memory; another frame of RAM, the kernel's or a process's, is
.Er EPERM .
A read or a write that comes to such a frame stops before it, and fails
with
.Er EPERM
when it is the first.
.Pp
A mapping must be shared, and its pages are mapped when it is made, with
the processor's cache disabled for them, so a load or a store reaches the
device.
A child of
.Xr fork 2
shares them.
The mapping does not grow with
.Xr mremap 2 ,
which fails with
.Er EFAULT ,
.Xr madvise 2
does not let its pages go and fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
and a core file and
.Pa /proc/PID/mem
leave its bytes out, since reading a device's register can change the
device;
.Pa /proc/PID/smaps
gives it the flags
.Ql io
and
.Ql pf .
.Sh FILES
.Pa /dev/mem
has mode 0640, owner and group root, and major 1, minor 1.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged, or the range has a frame of RAM past the
first megabyte.
.It Er EINVAL
A private mapping.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-miscutils 1 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr null 4
